# The Xiaomi camera's extra modes are Qigsaw dynamic feature splits, not code in the APK.
# Selecting Panorama, Clone, Vlog, Movie lens, Mimoji, Mi Live or Ambilight makes the app
# download a split APK, unpack its native libraries under
# /data/data/com.android.camera/app_qigsaw/<qigsawId>/<split>/<version>/nativeLib/arm64-v8a and
# dlopen() them. The app is signed with the platform key, so it runs as platform_app with its
# data labelled app_data_file, and this policy grants that pair everything except execute:
#
#   dlopen failed: couldn't map ".../nativeLib/arm64-v8a/libmorpho_sensor_fusion.so"
#   segment 2: Permission denied
#
# Qigsaw treats the load failure as a bad install and deletes the whole module, which is why
# every module directory on the phone is empty and why the modes look like they never download.
# The download itself works: the split's md5 matches the manifest inside the APK.
#
# This is a W^X relaxation and it is deliberate. It lets any platform-key-signed app run native
# code it wrote into its own data directory; on this build that is the camera, SystemUI,
# PackageInstaller, bthelper and flipendo, all signed by the same key as the rest of the image.
# The narrower alternatives were rejected: a private data type for the camera alone would drop
# it out of the many platform rules that name app_data_file by hand, and a private domain would
# have to re-derive everything platform_app grants the camera.
#
# execmod is deliberately not granted: none of the nine libraries in the panorama split carries
# a text relocation, so mapping them needs execute only.
# See config/nezha-camera-split-modules.json and docs/camera-split-modules-20260911.md.
allow platform_app app_data_file:file execute;
